*Feature Translation: MCP Cools, just One Year after its Birth
<https://substack.com/redirect/55723bfd-e7aa-44a9-9b5e-e667295a5013?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*

*Context: *In November 2024, Anthropic released a technology update that
largely escaped the headlines. Anthropic=E2=80=99s Model Context Protocol (=
MCP)
acts like a USB port for AI applications. Just like your computer=E2=80=99s=
 USB
port allows it to exchange data with other devices (e.g., a phone) as well
as connect to add-ons (e.g., a mouse, an external monitor), MCP provides a
*standardized* set of procedures that enable one company=E2=80=99s AI agent=
s to
connect to data sources and tools from other companies.

The MCP standard could develop into a globally interoperable interface that
allows AI model builders to connect with developers of complementary
products, thereby growing the overall AI ecosystem. The potential economies
of scale have attracted even Anthropic=E2=80=99s fiercest rivals to adopt t=
he
protocol. In fact, Chinese AI giants Baidu, Alibaba, and Tencent all
embraced MCP before Google and OpenAI *followed suit
<https://substack.com/redirect/f9158678-b939-4782-9590-7860ab0735d8?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*.
There are numerous tutorials that guide users through employing MCP to link
their main AI app with China=E2=80=99s DeepSeek R1 model and take advantage=
 of its
advanced reasoning capabilities.

In this week=E2=80=99s translation of a Gui Xingren [=E7=A1=85=E6=98=9F=E4=
=BA=BA] article (link to *original
Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/699ff0e7-4552-4609-a5bd-509e5ba85c0f?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
Dongdao Li provides an one-year-in update on MCP. Previously, I=E2=80=99ve
translated a Gui Xingren analysis of Chinese procurement data to evaluate
the diffusion of large models to government-facing applications (*ChinAI
#275
<https://substack.com/redirect/a2fd43aa-fe3a-4b1d-8446-c59aa922ec95?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*).


*Key Takeaways: The promise of MCP channels why technical standards are
essential infrastructure for modular technology ecosystems. *

   -

   Li explains: =E2=80=9C*Before MCP, if developers wanted Claude to access=
 Google
   Drive, they had to write a set of code to connect to the API. If they
   wanted it to access Slack? They had to write another API. It was even wo=
rse
   for IDE (integrated development environment) vendors; Cursor (popular AI
   code editor) had to be adapted to Linear (a project management tool),
   Windsurf (another AI coding assistant) had to be adapted to Linear, and
   everyone was doing almost the exact same dirty work*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   In the MCP Era, Linear can just build one MCP server,, and any AI agent,
   developed by any company, can plug in.

*However, when promise meets real-world engineering implementation, MPC has
cooled off.*

   -

   Li writes, =E2=80=9CEveryone knows how expensive Claude=E2=80=99s tokens=
 are; loading
   too many MCPs before even generating any content results in significant
   costs.=E2=80=9D The article cites a Simon Willison blog post (linked in =
Four to
   Forward section), which points out that GitHub=E2=80=99s official MCP in=
cludes 93
   different tools that cost 55,000 tokens.
   -

   When AI agents have to call too many MCPs, they can malfunction and
   hallucinate. In some cases, they have accidentally deleted codebases. Li
   also references a research preprint that analyzed around 1,900 MCP serve=
rs,
   finding numerous issues such as lack of maintenance and exposed credenti=
als.

<https://substack.com/redirect/ee5c64ae-5dc5-4f1b-90c1-82ef141d36f5?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>

   -

   The bigger picture, according to Li: =E2=80=9C*We are using deterministi=
c
   engineering methods to try and control a probabilistic intelligent agent=
*.
   *Imagine the day when a truly powerful ASI (Artificial
   Superintelligence) emerges. Will it still need you to write an MCP serve=
r
   to tell it how to check the weather? No, it will open a browser and take=
 a
   look. It doesn=E2=80=99t need protocols because it is the adapter itself=
.*=E2=80=9D

**One last note that I thought readers might find interesting, especially
those who use machine translation in their research. *

   -

   I always pop the Chinese text into Google Translate as a first pass.
   This week, I noticed something new. Google Translate automatically added=
 a
   parenthetical explanation for Xiaohongshu (a Chinese social media
   platform). This additional tag is not in the original Chinese, as most
   Chinese readers would know the popular platform. While it helped provide
   context in this case, Google Translate also added a parenthetical
   explanation for the MCP acronym, but this time the addition was incorrec=
t
   and misleading (Multi-Channel Programming) =E2=80=94 when MCP obviously =
refers to
   Model Context Protocol in this case. It will be interesting to track how
   machine translation platforms continue to change.

