n>The bigger picture, according to Li: =E2=80=9C</span><em>We are using det=
erministic engineering methods to try and control a probabilistic intellige=
nt agent</em><span>. </span><em>Imagine the day when a truly powerful ASI (=
Artificial Superintelligence) emerges. Will it still need you to write an M=
CP server to tell it how to check the weather? No, it will open a browser a=
nd take a look. It doesn=E2=80=99t need protocols because it is the adapter=
 itself.</em><span>=E2=80=9D</span></p></li></ul><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20p=
x 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>*One last =
note that I thought readers might find interesting, especially those who us=
e machine translation in their research. </strong></p><ul style=3D"margin-t=
op:0;padding:0"><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,=
55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:=
4px;font-size:16px;margin:0">I always pop the Chinese text into Google Tran=
slate as a first pass. This week, I noticed something new. Google Translate=
 automatically added a parenthetical explanation for Xiaohongshu (a Chinese=
 social media platform). This additional tag is not in the original Chinese=
, as most Chinese readers would know the popular platform. While it helped =
provide context in this case, Google Translate also added a parenthetical e=
xplanation for the MCP acronym, but this time the addition was incorrect an=
d misleading (Multi-Channel Programming) =E2=80=94 when MCP obviously refer=
s to Model Context Protocol in this case. It will be interesting to track h=
ow machine translation platforms continue to change.</p></li></ul><p style=
=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px">=
<strong><span>FULL TRANSLATION includes some cheeky WeChat comments from Ch=
